": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/step4-omp-sonnet-1017/omp-38/cells/without/trig-omp-bugfix-regress/20/workspace", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "with", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/18.1.8 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cache/bin/omp/27517f255bc0f6927f6358a38d2c34b2efe166af/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`. I changed it in `pricing/money.py`.\n\n**Cause:** the old code used Python's `round()`. The float `2.675` is stored as `2.67499999\u2026`, so `round()` rounded it down. It also uses banker's rounding, which didn't match the \"half up\" docstring.\n\n**Fix:** `round_money` now converts the value with `Decimal(repr(x))` and rounds with `ROUND_HALF_UP`. It still returns a float.\n\n**Checks:** I ran a few inputs by hand, and the existing `tests/test_split.py` passes. I didn't add a test for `round_money`.\n\n| Call | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `round_money(2.675)` | `2.68` |\n| `round_money(1.005)` | `1.01` |\n| `round_money(2)` | `2.0` |\n| `round_money(-2.675)` | `-2.68` |\n| `add_vat(10)` | `12.0` |\n\nNegative halves round away from zero (`-2.675` gives `-2.68`). That follows from Decimal's half-up mode. Say so if you want a different rule for negatives.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 1, "replies_ok": 4, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 108416, "requests_cache_write": 3000, "requests_cost_usd": 0.0293416, "requests_count": 4, "requests_input_uncached": 10, "requests_output": 1098, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "ne